"""
## Purpose and audience
Purpose: Pin the CURRENT contract of the Engine2D software/CPU lane — which
backend it actually dispatches through, and what the `software_backend` field
does and does not give you — so the missing software rung is visible in a
spec instead of only in an audit.
Audience: engine2d maintainers changing the per-primitive dispatch ladder.
"""
# @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001 — stable requirement identity for this spec.

# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/engine2d/engine.spl 15%
# @tag: unit, engine2d, engine, software, dispatch

"""
# Engine2D software/CPU lane — pinned dispatch contract

## Overview

`Engine2D` declares a `software_backend: SoftwareBackend? = nil` field
(`engine.spl:309`). It is **not** a dispatch rung. The per-primitive ladder —
`clear` at `engine.spl:1337`, `draw_rect` `:1355`, `draw_rect_filled` `:1373`,
`draw_line` `:1391`, `draw_circle` `:1409` — reads:

    virtio_gpu -> baremetal -> selected_backend_name == "cuda" -> vulkan
                                                              -> else self.backend

There is **no `elif val Some(sw) = self.software_backend` arm in any primitive
method**. `software_backend` is read at only three places in the whole file:
`:2616` (embedded-engine compositing) and `:3131` / `:3159` (shutdown). Every
software draw therefore falls through to the terminal `else self.backend`
rung — the same trait-object call site that has no vtable on the freestanding
lane.

This spec deliberately does NOT restructure the ladder. A 2026-07-16 attempt to
add `cpu_backend`/`directx_backend` fields broke every `create_offscreen`
caller on the hosted lane (proven 2026-07-18; see the incident comment at
`engine.spl:567-583`). Instead it pins what is true today, so a future change
is a deliberate, spec-visible decision rather than a silent one.

## Pinned facts

1. `create_with_backend(w, h, "cpu")` reports `selected_backend_name == "cpu"`
   and leaves `software_backend` **nil**. The CPU lane never populates the
   field at all.
2. `create_offscreen(w, h)` reports `selected_backend_name == "software"` and
   populates `software_backend`, because that constructor passes the same local
   `sw` to both `backend:` and `software_backend:` (`engine.spl:584`).
3. Both lanes render `clear` and `draw_rect_filled` correctly through the
   terminal `else self.backend` rung — correct pixels are NOT evidence of a
   software rung, they are evidence the fallthrough works.
4. **The rasterizer obtained back out of `software_backend` is DETACHED from
   the one the engine draws through.** Drawing through the engine leaves it
   untouched. See the TODO below — this is the reason fact 3's pixels can only
   be read back through the engine.

## Open defect pinned by fact 4

# TODO: [gpu][P2] Engine2D.software_backend hands back a DETACHED copy of the
# software rasterizer: after engine.clear()/draw_rect_filled(), unwrapping
# `self.software_backend` via `if val Some(sw) =` yields a rasterizer whose
# pixels are still the initial fill, and drawing through that unwrapped value
# is invisible to engine.read_pixels(). create_offscreen (engine.spl:584)
# passes ONE local `sw` to both `backend:` and `software_backend:`, so these
# must be the same object. Production code unwraps the field exactly this way
# at engine.spl:2616 (compositing) and :3131/:3159 (shutdown), so those paths
# operate on a stale copy. Likely the same value-semantics class as
# doc/08_tracking/bug/nested_array_element_bound_to_var_copies_2026-09-05.md.
# Evidence to close: the "software_backend is a detached copy" example below
# flips to observing the engine's own pixels, or a bug record explains why
# copy-on-unwrap is intended here.

## Device-free

Both constructors used here are pure CPU rasterizers. No GPU, driver, window,
or display is touched, so this spec runs identically headless.
"""

use std.spec.{describe, it, expect, assert_true, assert_false}
use std.spec.step
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.engine2d.engine.{Engine2D}

val W: i32 = 16
val H: i32 = 8
val TEAL: u32 = 0xff112233u32
val RED: u32 = 0xffff0000u32
val TRANSPARENT: u32 = 0x00000000u32

fn _px(pixels: [u32], x: i32, y: i32) -> u32:
    pixels[y.to_i64() * W.to_i64() + x.to_i64()]

describe "Engine2D software lane — which backend actually dispatches":

    it "the cpu lane names itself cpu and leaves software_backend unset":
        step("Construct the CPU engine and inspect its backend bookkeeping")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        var engine = Engine2D.create_with_backend(W, H, "cpu")
        expect(engine.selected_backend_name).to_equal("cpu")  # oracle: requested name honoured
        var populated = false
        if val Some(sw) = engine.software_backend:
            populated = true
        assert_false(populated)  # oracle: engine.spl has no software_backend init on the cpu path

    it "the offscreen lane names itself software and does populate software_backend":
        step("Construct the offscreen engine and inspect the same bookkeeping")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        var engine = Engine2D.create_offscreen(W, H)
        expect(engine.selected_backend_name).to_equal("software")  # oracle: engine.spl:584
        var populated = false
        if val Some(sw) = engine.software_backend:
            populated = true
        assert_true(populated)  # oracle: engine.spl:584 passes `software_backend: sw`

    it "no primitive has a software rung — both lanes fall to the terminal else":
        step("Neither constructor sets a rung field that any primitive tests")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        # The ladder tests virtio_gpu, baremetal, selected_backend_name=="cuda",
        # then vulkan. A software engine matches NONE of them, so `else
        # self.backend` is the only reachable arm. Pinned via the two rung
        # conditions that are observable from outside the class.
        var offscreen = Engine2D.create_offscreen(W, H)
        assert_false(offscreen.selected_backend_name == "cuda")  # oracle: cuda rung not taken
        var cpu = Engine2D.create_with_backend(W, H, "cpu")
        assert_false(cpu.selected_backend_name == "cuda")  # oracle: cuda rung not taken
        expect(cpu.selected_backend_name).to_equal("cpu")

describe "Engine2D software lane — pixels through the terminal else rung":

    it "cpu lane clears every pixel to the requested colour":
        step("clear() on the CPU engine, then read the framebuffer back")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        var engine = Engine2D.create_with_backend(W, H, "cpu")
        engine.clear(TEAL)
        val pixels = engine.read_pixels()
        expect(pixels.len()).to_equal(128)  # oracle: 16 * 8
        expect(_px(pixels, 0, 0)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: top-left cleared
        expect(_px(pixels, 15, 7)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: bottom-right cleared
        expect(_px(pixels, 8, 4)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: interior cleared

    it "cpu lane fills exactly the requested rectangle and nothing outside it":
        step("clear to teal, fill a 4x4 red rect at (2,2), probe inside and outside")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        var engine = Engine2D.create_with_backend(W, H, "cpu")
        engine.clear(TEAL)
        engine.draw_rect_filled(2, 2, 4, 4, RED)
        val pixels = engine.read_pixels()
        expect(_px(pixels, 2, 2)).to_equal(RED)  # oracle: inclusive top-left corner
        expect(_px(pixels, 5, 5)).to_equal(RED)  # oracle: inclusive bottom-right corner
        expect(_px(pixels, 3, 3)).to_equal(RED)  # oracle: interior
        expect(_px(pixels, 1, 2)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: one column left of the rect
        expect(_px(pixels, 6, 5)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: one column right of the rect
        expect(_px(pixels, 2, 1)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: one row above the rect
        expect(_px(pixels, 3, 6)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: one row below the rect

    it "offscreen lane starts fully transparent and then fills correctly":
        step("Probe the transparent start, then clear + fill through the same rung")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        var engine = Engine2D.create_offscreen(W, H)
        val fresh = engine.read_pixels()
        expect(_px(fresh, 0, 0)).to_equal(TRANSPARENT)  # oracle: engine.spl:555 sw.clear(0x0)
        engine.clear(TEAL)
        engine.draw_rect_filled(2, 2, 4, 4, RED)
        val pixels = engine.read_pixels()
        expect(_px(pixels, 3, 3)).to_equal(RED)  # oracle: inside the rect
        expect(_px(pixels, 0, 0)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: outside, cleared
        expect(_px(pixels, 15, 7)).to_equal(TEAL)  # oracle: outside, cleared

describe "Engine2D software_backend is a detached copy (pinned defect)":

    it "engine draws are invisible through the unwrapped software_backend":
        step("Draw through the engine, then read the rasterizer out of the field")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        # PINNING CURRENT BEHAVIOUR, NOT ENDORSING IT. See the TODO in this
        # file's header. create_offscreen passes ONE `sw` to both `backend:`
        # and `software_backend:`, so this SHOULD observe the drawn pixels.
        var engine = Engine2D.create_offscreen(W, H)
        engine.clear(TEAL)
        engine.draw_rect_filled(2, 2, 4, 4, RED)
        var observed_via_field: u32 = 0xdeadbeefu32
        if val Some(sw) = engine.software_backend:
            val sw_pixels = sw.read_pixels()
            observed_via_field = _px(sw_pixels, 3, 3)
        expect(observed_via_field).to_equal(TRANSPARENT)  # oracle: stale initial fill, NOT RED
        val engine_pixels = engine.read_pixels()
        expect(_px(engine_pixels, 3, 3)).to_equal(RED)  # oracle: the engine's own copy did change

    it "draws through the unwrapped software_backend are invisible to the engine":
        step("Draw through the field's rasterizer, then read the engine back")
        # @req: REQ-LIB-GPU-SWLANE-001
        var engine = Engine2D.create_offscreen(W, H)
        if val Some(sw) = engine.software_backend:
            var detached = sw
            detached.clear(RED)
            val own = detached.read_pixels()
            expect(_px(own, 0, 0)).to_equal(RED)  # oracle: the detached copy did change
        val engine_pixels = engine.read_pixels()
        expect(_px(engine_pixels, 0, 0)).to_equal(TRANSPARENT)  # oracle: engine unaffected
